      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Although low back pain (LBP) is an important issue for the health profession, few studies have examined LBP among occupational therapy students."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To investigate the prevalence and distribution of LBP, its adverse sequelae; and to identify potential risk factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In 2005, a self-reported questionnaire was administered to occupational therapy students in Northern Queensland."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "FINDINGS",
          "content": "The 72-month period-prevalence of LBP was 64.6%. Nearly half (46.9%) had experienced pain for over 2 days, 38.8% suffered LBP that affected their daily lives, and 24.5% had sought medical treatment. The prevalence of LBP ranged from 45.5 to 77.1% (p = 0.004), while the prevalence of LBP symptoms persisting longer than two days was 34.1 to 62.5% (p = 0.020). Logistic regression analysis indicated that year of study and weekly computer usage were statistically-significant LBP risk factors."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "IMPLICATIONS",
          "content": "The occupational therapy profession will need to further investigate the high prevalence of student LBP identified in this study."
        }
      ]},
